% IF and ABORT — the whole of SEL's control flow.

### name: ctl.if.true-branch
--- source
IF(1 > 0, "yes", "no")
--- expect
text "yes"
===
### name: ctl.if.false-branch
--- source
IF(1 > 2, "yes", "no")
--- expect
text "no"
===
### name: ctl.if.two-arg-false-yields-empty-text
--- source
IF(1 > 2, "yes")
--- expect
text ""
===
### name: ctl.if.untaken-branch-not-evaluated
--- note
The point of the AST calling convention: the untaken branch never runs, so its
error never happens.
--- source
IF(1 > 0, "safe", 1 / 0)
--- expect
text "safe"
===
### name: ctl.if.condition-must-be-bool
--- source
IF(1, "yes", "no")
--- expect
error E_NOT_BOOL at 1:4
===
### name: ctl.if.side-effect-happens-once
--- source
N = 0; IF(TRUE, N = N + 1, N = N + 100); N
--- expect
num 1
===
### name: ctl.if.sequence-branch-needs-parens
--- note
`;` binds looser than `,`, so a sequenced branch takes its own parentheses.
--- source
IF(TRUE, (A = 1; A + 1), 0)
--- expect
num 2
===
### name: ctl.if.arity
--- source
IF(TRUE)
--- expect
error E_ARITY at 1:1
===
### name: ctl.if.constant-condition-result-keeps-the-if-position
--- note
`IF(TRUE, "x", 1)` is the IF's value: when `>=` rejects it, the node that
actually failed (spec §6.3) is the operand `>=` was handed -- the IF at 1:1 --
not the literal the IF chose. An optimiser that folds a constant-condition IF
to its branch must stamp the branch with the IF's position, and it can only do
that for a leaf literal; C++ never folded IF and so reported 1:1 while the four
hosts that did reported 1:10, which is the disagreement that pinned this.
--- source
IF(TRUE, "x", 1) >= 1
--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM at 1:1
===
### name: ctl.if.constant-false-condition-result-keeps-the-if-position
--- source
IF(FALSE, 1, "x") + 1
--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM at 1:1
===
### name: ctl.if.constant-condition-null-result-keeps-the-if-position
--- note
A NULL branch is a leaf too, and E_NULL is raised by `+` against its operand,
the IF.
--- source
IF(TRUE, NULL, 1) + 1
--- expect
error E_NULL at 1:1
===
### name: ctl.if.nested-constant-conditions-keep-the-outer-if-position
--- note
The inner IF folds to `"x"` at the inner IF's column, and that literal is a
leaf the outer IF may fold in turn -- to the outer IF's column, 1:1. Whatever
the optimiser does, `>=` was handed the outer IF.
--- source
IF(TRUE, IF(TRUE, "x", 1), 2) >= 1
--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM at 1:1
===
### name: ctl.if.constant-condition-compound-branch-keeps-its-own-positions
--- note
The limit of the fold: a compound branch has positions of its own inside it,
and the `/` that fails is at 1:12 whether or not the IF is still there. A fold
that hoisted the whole branch and stamped it would report 1:1, so a branch
that is not a leaf is left where it is.
--- source
IF(TRUE, 1 / 0, 2)
--- expect
error E_DIV_ZERO at 1:12
===
### name: ctl.if.constant-condition-variable-branch-is-the-if-value
--- note
A variable is a leaf that can fail on its own (E_UNDEF_VAR at its own column),
so it is not folded either; the value it holds is still the IF's when `+`
rejects it, and the IF is at 1:1.
--- setup
X = "x"
--- source
IF(TRUE, X, 1) + 1
--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM at 1:1
===
% COND — flat multi-branch selection, with IF's laziness and a mandatory default.

### name: ctl.cond.first-match
--- source
COND(TRUE, "a", TRUE, "b", "z")
--- expect
text "a"
===
### name: ctl.cond.later-match
--- source
COND(FALSE, "a", TRUE, "b", "z")
--- expect
text "b"
===
### name: ctl.cond.default
--- source
COND(FALSE, "a", FALSE, "b", "z")
--- expect
text "z"
===
### name: ctl.cond.ordering
--- setup
SCORE = 85
--- source
COND(SCORE >= 90, "A", SCORE >= 80, "B", SCORE >= 70, "C", "F")
--- expect
text "B"
===
### name: ctl.cond.untaken-result-not-evaluated
--- source
COND(TRUE, "safe", TRUE, 1 / 0, 1 / 0)
--- expect
text "safe"
===
### name: ctl.cond.conditions-after-match-not-evaluated
--- source
COND(TRUE, "safe", 1 / 0 == 0, "x", "z")
--- expect
text "safe"
===
### name: ctl.cond.default-not-evaluated-when-matched
--- source
COND(TRUE, "safe", ABORT("never"))
--- expect
text "safe"
===
### name: ctl.cond.side-effect-happens-once
--- source
N = 0; COND(FALSE, N = N + 100, TRUE, N = N + 1, N = N + 500); N
--- expect
num 1
===
### name: ctl.cond.condition-must-be-bool
--- source
COND(1, "a", "z")
--- expect
error E_NOT_BOOL at 1:6
===
### name: ctl.cond.even-arity-rejected
--- note
An even count would let one miscounted comma shift every pair by one and still
compile, so the default is mandatory and its absence is a compile-time error.
--- source
COND(TRUE, "a", FALSE, "b")
--- expect
error E_ARITY at 1:1
===
### name: ctl.cond.too-few-arguments
--- source
COND(TRUE, "a")
--- expect
error E_ARITY at 1:1
===
### name: ctl.cond.nested-in-aggregate
--- source
JOIN(MAP((0, 5, 200), COND(_ == 0, "empty", _ > 99, "bulk", "ok")), ",")
--- expect
text "empty,ok,bulk"
===
### name: ctl.cond.equivalent-to-if-ladder
--- setup
X = 3
--- source
COND(X == 1, "one", X == 2, "two", "many") $== IF(X == 1, "one", IF(X == 2, "two", "many"))
--- expect
bool TRUE
===

### name: ctl.abort
--- source
ABORT("nope")
--- expect
error E_ABORT
===
### name: ctl.abort.not-reached
--- source
IF(FALSE, ABORT("nope"), "fine")
--- expect
text "fine"
===
### name: ctl.unknown-function
--- source
NOSUCH(1)
--- expect
error E_UNKNOWN_FUNC at 1:1
===
### name: ctl.arity-checked-at-compile-time
--- note
Arity is a compile-time error, so it fires even inside a branch that would
never run.
--- source
IF(FALSE, LEN(), "fine")
--- expect
error E_ARITY
===
### name: assign.target-index-has-side-effects
--- note
The index expressions of an assignment target are evaluated, once each, left to
right, before the right-hand side. In C++ this crashed: the walk held a pointer
into the container's child vector across an evaluation that could grow it, and
the pointer dangled. Found by review, not by the fuzzer, because the generator
never emitted an assignment whose target had side effects.
--- source
A[(Z = "1")][1] = 5
--- expect
num 5
===
### name: assign.right-hand-side-rebuilds-the-target
--- note
The right-hand side may destroy the levels the target walk just created. The
assignment must still complete rather than dereferencing a level that has gone.
--- source
A[1][1] = (A = 2)
--- expect
num 2
===
### name: assign.base-exists-before-the-index-runs
--- note
Assigning to A[k] creates A first, so an index expression that reads A sees the
empty value the walk just created — the level creation is interleaved with the
key evaluation, not done before or after it.
--- source
A[COUNT(A)] = 1; A
--- expect
tree -{"0"=t"1"}
===
### name: assign.stores-at-the-path-not-the-old-container
--- note
The right-hand side replaced A entirely. The assignment stores into the A that
exists when the store happens — it is not silently discarded into the value A
used to be. See spec/SPEC.md §5.7.
--- source
A[1] = (A = 2); A
--- expect
tree t"2"{"1"=t"2"}
===
### name: assign.index-expr-replaces-the-container
--- source
A = (1, 2); A[(A = 5)] = 9; A
--- expect
tree t"5"{"5"=t"9"}
===
### name: assign.index-expr-rebuilds-the-container
--- source
A[(A=(9,9))["1"]] = 3; COUNT(A)
--- expect
num 3
===
### name: assign.compound-target-replaced-by-rhs
--- note
The compound form reads the target before the right-hand side runs, so the
arithmetic uses the old value, but the store still lands at the path.
--- source
A = 1; A += (A = 5); A
--- expect
num 6
===
